# Platform Restrictions & Known Limitations

This file documents features that **do not work** in our environment due to hardware, virtualisation, or platform constraints. Check here before suggesting or implementing GPU-intensive or platform-specific features.

---

## GPU Environment

- **GPU**: NVIDIA L40S-48Q (vGPU — virtual GPU profile, 48 GB VRAM, Compute Capability 8.9)
- **Virtualisation**: The `-Q` suffix indicates a **virtual workstation** profile running inside a VM with NVIDIA vGPU software.
- **Kit version**: 110.0.0

---

## Blocked: Gaussian Splatting / NuRec Rendering in Omniverse

**Status**: NOT WORKING on vGPU

**What it is**: Kit 110.0 added Neural Rendering (NuRec) support for 3D Gaussian Splats via `OmniNuRecVolumeAPI` on `UsdVol::Volume` prims. Gaussian Splat `.ply` files must be converted to `.usdz` with the NuRec schema (using [3DGRUT](https://github.com/nv-tlabs/3dgrut) `ply_to_usd.py` or the standalone [PLY_to_USD](https://github.com/TimChen1383/PLY_to_USD) converter).

**Why it fails**: NuRec uses `carb.cudainterop.plugin` to allocate GPU memory through CUDA-graphics API interoperability (sharing memory between CUDA and DirectX). vGPU environments **do not support CUDA-graphics interop**, producing:

```
CUDA error 801: cudaErrorNotSupported - operation not supported
Failed to allocate CUDA device memory (size: 8)
NuRec Device Memory Allocator: Failed to allocate 8 bytes via cudaInterop on device 0
```

This crashes Composer (exit code 3489660927). The 8-byte allocation size confirms it is not a memory limit — the API call itself is blocked.

**Workarounds investigated**:
- Importing raw `.ply` → shows as black dots (no NuRec schema, just point cloud)
- Converting `.ply` → `.usdz` with NuRec schema → crashes on `cudaInterop`
- Poisson mesh reconstruction from splat positions → poor quality ("lump of clay"), SH-to-vertex-color transfer loses all view-dependent detail
- OBJ export with vertex colors → Omniverse ignores OBJ vertex colors
- USD export with `displayColor` primvar → geometry is poor (Poisson from volumetric Gaussians ≠ surface reconstruction)

**What would fix it**:
- **GPU passthrough** instead of vGPU (full CUDA interop support)
- A different vGPU profile that enables CUDA-graphics interop (requires infrastructure/IT change)
- Future Kit update that avoids `cudaInterop` for NuRec allocation

**Alternatives that DO work**:
- **Gaussian Splat viewer (nerfstudio)**: `ns-viewer` at `localhost:7007` renders splats beautifully — runs on CUDA directly, no graphics interop needed
- **Web viewers**: [Playcanvas SuperSplat](https://playcanvas.com/supersplat/editor), [antimatter15](https://antimatter15.com/splat/), [Three.js viewer](https://mkkellogg.github.io/GaussianSplats3D/) — drag-and-drop `.ply`
- **Polycam**: Upload source video → get proper textured mesh (OBJ/GLTF/USDZ) via their photogrammetry pipeline
- **Nerfacto + TSDF**: Train a NeRF model (not Gaussian Splat) with `ns-train nerfacto`, then `ns-export tsdf` produces a proper mesh that Omniverse can render as standard geometry

**Gaussian Splat lab location**: `D:\Dev\gaussian-splat-lab\` — isolated conda environment with nerfstudio, gsplat, COLMAP, FFmpeg. Activate via `activate.bat`.

